Object

The primary objective of SQL Server memory monitoring is to ensure that SQL Server has sufficient memory to execute queries efficiently, cache frequently accessed data, manage execution plans, and support concurrent database workloads.

Memory is one of the most important resources in SQL Server. Improper memory configuration or memory pressure can cause slow query execution, excessive physical reads, query memory grant waits, and overall application performance degradation.

Key objectives

Monitor SQL Server memory utilization.

Identify SQL Server memory consumption at instance level.

Monitor Buffer Pool utilization.

Identify memory pressure inside SQL Server.

Monitor query memory grants.

Identify queries waiting for memory.

Monitor Plan Cache and Buffer Cache.

Identify memory-intensive queries and execution plans.

Monitor Windows OS memory availability.

Identify memory-related waits and errors.

Configure appropriate SQL Server max server memory.

Prevent production incidents caused by insufficient memory.

Expected outcome: A DBA should be able to identify whether SQL Server is experiencing memory pressure, determine which component is consuming memory, identify affected queries, and implement an appropriate corrective action.

