Scope boundaries

A DBA should understand the difference between the following:

OS Memory Pressure: Windows does not have sufficient available physical memory.

SQL Server Memory Pressure: SQL Server cannot efficiently satisfy internal memory requirements.

Buffer Pool Pressure: SQL Server cannot retain sufficient data pages in memory.

Query Memory Pressure: Queries require workspace memory for operations such as sorting and hashing.

Plan Cache Pressure: Cached execution plans are removed or reused due to memory management.

External Memory Pressure: Other applications or processes compete with SQL Server for RAM.

Important: High SQL Server memory consumption is not automatically a problem. SQL Server is designed to use available memory to cache data and improve performance.



  Introdution and Overview



SQL Server uses memory to store frequently accessed data pages, execution plans, internal structures, query workspace allocations, and other engine components.

When an application executes a query, SQL Server attempts to retrieve the required data from memory rather than reading it repeatedly from disk.

This improves query performance by reducing physical disk I/O.

Why does SQL Server need memory?

SQL Server primarily uses memory for:

Buffer Pool – Data and index pages.

Plan Cache – Cached execution plans.

Query Execution – Memory grants for sorting and hashing.

Lock Management – Lock-related structures.

Connection Management – Session-related memory.

Internal Engine Operations – Various internal allocations.

Columnstore – Columnstore object and segment memory.

In-Memory OLTP – Memory-optimized tables and indexes, when configured.

