Important memory architecture components

Memory Manager

SQL Server Memory Manager manages memory allocations for the database engine.

Its responsibilities include:

Managing SQL Server memory consumption.

Handling memory requests from internal components.

Monitoring memory availability.

Responding to operating system memory pressure.

Managing memory within configured limits.

Buffer Pool

The Buffer Pool is one of the most important components of SQL Server memory.

It stores:

Data pages.

Index pages.

Database pages read from disk.

Modified pages (dirty pages) waiting to be written to disk.

Example:

Suppose an application executes:

SELECT *
FROM dbo.Employee
WHERE EmployeeID = 1001;

If the required data page is already present in the Buffer Pool, SQL Server can read it from memory.

If the page is not present, SQL Server reads it from the database files into memory.

This is why sufficient Buffer Pool memory can reduce physical disk reads.

Plan Cache

Plan Cache stores compiled execution plans.

When a query is executed again and SQL Server can reuse its cached plan, it may avoid compiling a new execution plan.

Example:

SELECT *
FROM dbo.Employee
WHERE EmployeeID = @EmployeeID;

A reusable execution plan can reduce compilation overhead.

However, excessive single-use ad hoc plans can consume memory unnecessarily.

Query Execution Memory / Workspace Memory

Certain query operators require additional workspace memory.

Examples:

Sort operations.

Hash joins.

Hash aggregation.

Other memory-consuming query operators.

SQL Server estimates the memory required for a query and requests a memory grant before executing the relevant operators.

If sufficient workspace memory is unavailable, the query might wait on RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE.

Memory Clerks

Memory Clerks track memory consumption by different SQL Server components.

Examples include:

Memory clerk

Purpose

MEMORYCLERK_SQLBUFFERPOOL

Buffer Pool

CACHESTORE_SQLCP

SQL plan cache

CACHESTORE_OBJCP

Object plan cache

MEMORYCLERK_SQLQUERYEXEC

Query execution

MEMORYCLERK_SQLGENERAL

General SQL Server allocations

MEMORYCLERK_SQLCLR

SQL CLR allocations

The exact clerk names and their usage depend on SQL Server version and workload.

