Collect basic SQL Server configuration

Query 1 – Check SQL Server version

SELECT
    @@SERVERNAME AS ServerName,
    @@VERSION AS SQLServerVersion;

Query 2 – Check physical and logical memory information

SELECT
    cpu_count AS LogicalCPUCount,
    physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS PhysicalMemory_MB,
    committed_kb / 1024 AS CommittedMemory_MB,
    committed_target_kb / 1024 AS CommittedTarget_MB,
    sqlserver_start_time
FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;

Query 3 – Check current memory configuration

SELECT
    name,
    value AS ConfiguredValue,
    value_in_use AS ActiveValue
FROM sys.configurations
WHERE name IN
(
    'min server memory (MB)',
    'max server memory (MB)'
);

Query 4 – Check Windows available memory

SELECT
    total_physical_memory_kb / 1024
        AS TotalPhysicalMemory_MB,

    available_physical_memory_kb / 1024
        AS AvailablePhysicalMemory_MB,

    system_memory_state_desc

FROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory;

Why is this important?

Before changing memory configuration, a DBA should understand:

Total physical RAM available.

Windows memory consumption.

SQL Server committed memory.

Current max server memory setting.

Other applications running on the server.

Number of SQL Server instances.

Whether the server uses NUMA architecture.

 Action

This is the most important section for a production SQL Server DBA.

The recommended approach is to investigate memory from the operating system level to the individual SQL Server component and query level.

Production memory troubleshooting workflow

Check Windows available physical memory.

Check SQL Server Total and Target Server Memory.

Check SQL Server process memory consumption.

Identify memory consumption by Memory Clerks.

Monitor Buffer Pool and Page Life Expectancy.

Check Memory Grants Pending and Outstanding.

Identify queries waiting for memory.

Analyze memory-related waits and errors.

Review max server memory and workload configuration.

Implement corrective actions and validate.

Action 1 – Check SQL Server Total and Target Memory

Easy DMV – Remember this first

DMV: sys.dm_os_sys_info

This DMV provides information about the SQL Server instance, including its current committed memory and target memory.

SELECT
    committed_kb / 1024
        AS TotalServerMemory_MB,

    committed_target_kb / 1024
        AS TargetServerMemory_MB,

    physical_memory_kb / 1024
        AS PhysicalMemory_MB

FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;

How to interpret the results:

Metric

Meaning

Total Server Memory

Currently committed memory

Target Server Memory

Memory SQL Server currently targets

Physical Memory

Physical RAM visible to SQL Server

Example:

Metric

Value

Physical Memory

64 GB

Total Server Memory

48 GB

Target Server Memory

48 GB

Interpretation:

SQL Server has reached its current memory target. This is not automatically a memory problem. Check Windows available memory, query memory grants, and memory pressure indicators.

Action 2 – Check SQL Server Process Memory

Easy DMV – Process monitoring

DMV: sys.dm_os_process_memory

This DMV provides information about memory used by the SQL Server operating system process.

SELECT
    physical_memory_in_use_kb / 1024
        AS SQL_Physical_Memory_MB,

    large_page_allocations_kb / 1024
        AS Large_Page_Allocations_MB,

    locked_page_allocations_kb / 1024
        AS Locked_Page_Allocations_MB,

    virtual_address_space_reserved_kb / 1024
        AS VAS_Reserved_MB,

    virtual_address_space_committed_kb / 1024
        AS VAS_Committed_MB,

    virtual_address_space_available_kb / 1024
        AS VAS_Available_MB,

    page_fault_count,

    memory_utilization_percentage,

    process_physical_memory_low,

    process_virtual_memory_low

FROM sys.dm_os_process_memory;

Important columns:

physical_memory_in_use_kb: Physical memory currently used by SQL Server process.

locked_page_allocations_kb: Memory allocated using locked pages.

page_fault_count: Page fault information.

process_physical_memory_low: Indicates SQL Server process physical memory pressure.

process_virtual_memory_low: Indicates virtual memory pressure.

Production scenario:

Windows team reports that SQL Server is consuming almost all available RAM.

DBA action:

Check SQL Server process memory.

Check Total and Target Server Memory.

Check Windows available memory.

Review max server memory.

Investigate memory clerks.

Determine whether memory consumption is expected or problematic.

Important: Task Manager process memory and SQL Server Buffer Pool memory are not identical. SQL Server process memory can include allocations outside the Buffer Pool limit.

Action 3 – Check Windows Operating System Memory

Easy DMV – OS monitoring

DMV: sys.dm_os_sys_memory

This DMV provides operating system memory information visible to SQL Server.

SELECT
    total_physical_memory_kb / 1024
        AS Total_RAM_MB,

    available_physical_memory_kb / 1024
        AS Available_RAM_MB,

    total_page_file_kb / 1024
        AS Total_PageFile_MB,

    available_page_file_kb / 1024
        AS Available_PageFile_MB,

    system_memory_state_desc,

    system_low_memory_signal_state,

    system_high_memory_signal_state

FROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory;

What should a DBA check?

Is available physical memory consistently low?

Is the operating system reporting low memory?

Is page file consumption increasing?

Is another application consuming excessive memory?

Is SQL Server competing with other instances?

Resolution: Investigate the process consuming memory before reducing SQL Server memory allocation.

Action 4 – Identify Memory Consumption by Memory Clerks

Most important DMV – Advanced analysis

DMV: sys.dm_os_memory_clerks

This DMV helps identify which internal SQL Server component is consuming the most memory.

SELECT TOP 20
    type AS MemoryClerkType,

    name AS MemoryClerkName,

    SUM(pages_kb) / 1024.0
        AS PagesMemory_MB,

    SUM(virtual_memory_committed_kb) / 1024.0
        AS VirtualMemoryCommitted_MB

FROM sys.dm_os_memory_clerks

GROUP BY
    type,
    name

ORDER BY PagesMemory_MB DESC;

Important memory clerks:

Memory Clerk

Purpose

MEMORYCLERK_SQLBUFFERPOOL

Buffer Pool memory

CACHESTORE_SQLCP

Ad hoc SQL plan cache

CACHESTORE_OBJCP

Stored procedure and object plan cache

MEMORYCLERK_SQLQUERYEXEC

Query execution allocations

MEMORYCLERK_SQLGENERAL

General SQL allocations

MEMORYCLERK_SQLCLR

CLR memory

Production interpretation:

Suppose you find:

Memory Clerk

Memory

SQLBUFFERPOOL

30 GB

SQLQUERYEXEC

5 GB

SQLCP

2 GB

SQLCLR

1 GB

Interpretation:

Buffer Pool is consuming the largest share.

Query execution memory is also significant.

Review active query memory grants.

Check whether plan cache consumption is justified.

Remember: Clerk values are not a complete accounting of process memory, and different clerk allocations should not be blindly added to Task Manager values.

Action 5 – Check Buffer Pool Memory

DMV: sys.dm_os_buffer_descriptors

This DMV provides information about database pages currently present in the Buffer Pool.

SELECT
    DB_NAME(database_id) AS DatabaseName,

    COUNT(*) AS CachedPages,

    COUNT(*) * 8 / 1024.0
        AS BufferPoolMemory_MB

FROM sys.dm_os_buffer_descriptors

WHERE database_id <> 32767

GROUP BY database_id

ORDER BY BufferPoolMemory_MB DESC;

What does this query tell us?

Which database is using the most Buffer Pool memory?

How much memory is occupied by cached pages?

Which database has a large in-memory footprint?

Example:

Database

Buffer Pool Memory

SalesDB

20 GB

FinanceDB

10 GB

HRDB

5 GB

This indicates the distribution of cached database pages, not necessarily which database is responsible for memory pressure.

Action 6 – Monitor Page Life Expectancy (PLE)

Important daily health-check counter

PerfMon counter: SQLServer:Buffer Manager – Page life expectancy

PLE estimates how long, in seconds, a data page is expected to remain in the Buffer Pool.

A sudden and sustained drop can indicate memory pressure or a significant change in workload.

Check PLE using T-SQL:

SELECT
    object_name,
    counter_name,
    instance_name,
    cntr_value AS PageLifeExpectancy_Seconds

FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters

WHERE counter_name = 'Page life expectancy';

Interpretation:

PLE behavior

Interpretation

Stable PLE

Buffer Pool residency is relatively stable

Sudden PLE drop

Possible memory or workload pressure

Repeated sharp drops

Investigate workload and memory churn

Low PLE with high physical reads

Stronger reason to investigate Buffer Pool pressure

Important: Do not blindly apply the historical 300-second PLE threshold to every server. Modern systems can have multiple NUMA nodes, different RAM sizes, and different workloads. Monitor PLE trends per memory node and compare against the normal baseline.

Action 7 – Check Query Memory Grants

Advanced DMV – Production troubleshooting

DMV: sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants

This DMV provides information about query workspace memory requested, granted, used, and waiting.

SELECT
    session_id,

    request_time,

    grant_time,

    requested_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS RequestedMemory_MB,

    granted_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS GrantedMemory_MB,

    required_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS RequiredMemory_MB,

    used_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS UsedMemory_MB,

    max_used_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS MaxUsedMemory_MB,

    wait_time_ms,

    wait_order,

    is_next_candidate

FROM sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants

ORDER BY requested_memory_kb DESC;

Important columns:

Column

Meaning

requested_memory_kb

Requested memory

granted_memory_kb

Memory actually granted

required_memory_kb

Minimum required memory

used_memory_kb

Current memory usage

max_used_memory_kb

Maximum memory used

wait_time_ms

Memory grant waiting time

grant_time

Time the grant was received

Production scenario:

Application team reports that several queries are running slowly.

DBA checks memory grants and identifies queries waiting for memory.

Possible reasons:

Large hash joins.

Large sort operations.

Excessive query concurrency.

Poor cardinality estimates.

Overestimated memory grants.

Resolution:

Review query execution plans.

Check actual versus estimated row counts.

Optimize joins and filters.

Investigate memory grant feedback.

Review concurrent workload.

Consider Resource Governor if workload isolation is required.

Action 8 – Identify Queries Waiting for Memory

DMVs:

sys.dm_exec_requests

sys.dm_exec_sql_text

sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants

SELECT
    r.session_id,

    r.status,

    r.wait_type,

    r.wait_time,

    r.granted_query_memory * 8 / 1024.0
        AS GrantedMemory_MB,

    r.total_elapsed_time,

    DB_NAME(r.database_id) AS DatabaseName,

    st.text AS SQLStatement

FROM sys.dm_exec_requests r

CROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text(r.sql_handle) st

WHERE r.wait_type = 'RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE'

ORDER BY r.wait_time DESC;

What does RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE mean?

It indicates that a query is waiting for a workspace memory grant.

When multiple queries request large memory grants concurrently, SQL Server might not have sufficient memory to grant all requests immediately.

Action 9 – Check Memory Grants Pending and Outstanding

DMV: sys.dm_os_performance_counters

SELECT
    counter_name,

    cntr_value

FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters

WHERE object_name LIKE '%:Memory Manager%'

AND counter_name IN
(
    'Memory Grants Pending',
    'Memory Grants Outstanding',
    'Total Server Memory (KB)',
    'Target Server Memory (KB)'
);

Interpretation:

Counter

Expected behavior

Memory Grants Pending

Ideally 0

Memory Grants Outstanding

Depends on workload

Total Server Memory

Current committed memory

Target Server Memory

Current memory target

Important: Memory Grants Pending remaining above zero for a sustained period should trigger investigation. A single nonzero sample is not sufficient to establish a production incident.

Action 10 – Check Plan Cache Memory Consumption

DMVs:

sys.dm_exec_cached_plans

sys.dm_os_memory_cache_counters

First, check cache memory:

SELECT
    type,

    name,

    pages_kb / 1024.0
        AS CacheMemory_MB,

    entries_count

FROM sys.dm_os_memory_cache_counters

ORDER BY pages_kb DESC;

Identify single-use ad hoc plans:

SELECT
    objtype,

    cacheobjtype,

    COUNT(*) AS PlanCount,

    SUM(size_in_bytes) / 1024.0 / 1024
        AS PlanCacheMemory_MB

FROM sys.dm_exec_cached_plans

GROUP BY
    objtype,
    cacheobjtype

ORDER BY PlanCacheMemory_MB DESC;

Possible issue:

Thousands of single-use ad hoc plans can consume unnecessary plan cache memory.

Resolution:

Parameterize repetitive queries.

Review application SQL generation.

Investigate unnecessary plan creation.

Evaluate optimize for ad hoc workloads after analyzing the workload.

Action 11 – Check SQL Server Memory-Related Wait Statistics

DMV: sys.dm_os_wait_stats

SELECT
    wait_type,

    waiting_tasks_count,

    wait_time_ms / 1000.0
        AS WaitTime_Seconds,

    signal_wait_time_ms / 1000.0
        AS SignalWait_Seconds

FROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats

WHERE wait_type IN
(
    'RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE',
    'RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE_QUERY_COMPILE',
    'MEMORY_ALLOCATION_EXT',
    'CMEMTHREAD'
)

ORDER BY wait_time_ms DESC;

Wait type

Meaning

Investigation

RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE

Query waiting for workspace memory

Memory grants

RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE_QUERY_COMPILE

Query compilation memory pressure

Compilation workload

MEMORY_ALLOCATION_EXT

Internal memory allocation waiting

Memory allocation pressure

CMEMTHREAD

Contention around memory object allocation

Memory object contention

Important: Wait statistics are cumulative and need to be compared across a defined observation interval. Not every memory-related wait indicates that the server needs more RAM.

Action 12 – Check SQL Server Memory Configuration and MAX Server Memory

Important – Production configuration

Purpose: Ensure SQL Server does not consume memory required by Windows or other applications.

Check the current settings:

SELECT
    name,
    value AS ConfiguredValue_MB,
    value_in_use AS ActiveValue_MB
FROM sys.configurations
WHERE name IN
(
    'min server memory (MB)',
    'max server memory (MB)'
);

How to configure MAX Server Memory

Example: Suppose you have calculated and approved 48 GB as the appropriate SQL Server memory limit.

EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;
RECONFIGURE;

EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', 49152;
RECONFIGURE;

Example only: 49,152 MB = 48 GB. Do not copy this value without calculating the requirements of your actual server.

Important considerations before changing MAX Server Memory:

Check total physical RAM.

Reserve sufficient memory for Windows.

Account for other SQL Server instances.

Account for backup buffers and other external allocations.

Review SQL Server process memory outside the configured limit.

Consider SQL Server workload and peak memory requirements.

Obtain change approval and monitor after implementation.

Microsoft provides a memory sizing methodology that accounts for OS requirements, thread stacks, and allocations outside the max server memory limit. A flat 75% allocation is only a rough starting point, not a universal rule.

Microsoft Learn+1



Action 13 – Check Memory Distribution Across NUMA Nodes

DMV: sys.dm_os_memory_nodes

On servers with NUMA architecture, SQL Server manages memory across multiple memory nodes.

SELECT
    memory_node_id,

    virtual_address_space_reserved_kb / 1024.0
        AS VAS_Reserved_MB,

    virtual_address_space_committed_kb / 1024.0
        AS VAS_Committed_MB,

    locked_page_allocations_kb / 1024.0
        AS LockedPages_MB,

    pages_kb / 1024.0
        AS Pages_MB

FROM sys.dm_os_memory_nodes

ORDER BY memory_node_id;

Why is this important?

Understand memory distribution across NUMA nodes.

Identify uneven memory allocation patterns.

Investigate NUMA-related performance issues.

Correlate memory behavior with scheduler and CPU topology.

Do not assume that uneven memory values automatically indicate a problem. Review the workload and NUMA configuration.

 Troubleshooting

A production DBA should not immediately restart SQL Server or increase RAM whenever memory utilization is high.

First identify the type of memory pressure.

Scenario 1 – SQL Server is consuming almost 95–100% of server RAM

High Priority

Problem statement:

Windows team reports that SQL Server is consuming almost all physical RAM, and other applications on the server are responding slowly.

Possible root causes:

SQL Server max server memory is not configured appropriately.

Buffer Pool has grown to its memory target.

Another SQL Server instance is consuming memory.

Memory allocations outside the configured limit are high.

Other Windows applications are consuming RAM.

Memory leak in an external component or application.

Troubleshooting steps:

Step

DBA action

1

Check Windows available RAM

2

Check SQL Server process memory

3

Check Total and Target Server Memory

4

Review MAX Server Memory

5

Identify top memory clerks

6

Check other applications and SQL instances

7

Review Windows memory pressure indicators

8

Apply an approved corrective action

Resolution:

Configure an appropriate MAX Server Memory value.

Investigate external memory allocations.

Review competing applications.

Add RAM if the legitimate workload exceeds capacity.

Monitor memory usage after the change.

Important: SQL Server consuming most of its configured memory is normally expected. The real issue is whether Windows or SQL Server is experiencing memory pressure.

Scenario 2 – Memory Grants Pending is continuously increasing

High Priority

Problem statement:

Application team reports slow query execution. DBA observes that Memory Grants Pending is increasing.

Possible root causes:

Large hash joins.

Large sorting operations.

Queries requesting excessive memory.

Concurrent memory-intensive queries.

Poor cardinality estimates.

Insufficient workspace memory.

Troubleshooting steps:

Check Memory Grants Pending in PerfMon.

Execute sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants.

Identify queries waiting for memory.

Check RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE waits.

Review actual execution plans.

Compare requested memory with actual usage.

Investigate concurrent workload.

Resolution:

Optimize expensive queries.

Reduce unnecessary sorting and hashing.

Improve cardinality estimates.

Review indexes and query design.

Evaluate memory grant feedback.

Increase available memory only if the workload genuinely requires it.

Scenario 3 – Page Life Expectancy suddenly drops

Problem statement:

The DBA observes that PLE was stable earlier but suddenly dropped from 2,000 seconds to 100 seconds.

Possible reasons:

Large reporting queries.

Index rebuild operations.

Large table scans.

Memory pressure.

Workload spikes.

Sudden increase in data access.

Troubleshooting steps:

Check PLE trend.

Check Buffer Pool counters.

Check Windows available memory.

Identify queries with high logical reads.

Review recent maintenance jobs.

Check memory clerks.

Compare the current workload with the normal baseline.

Resolution:

Optimize large scans.

Review indexing strategy.

Schedule maintenance appropriately.

Investigate workload changes.

Add memory if capacity is genuinely insufficient.

Remember: PLE dropping is a symptom, not a diagnosis. A single PLE value cannot confirm memory pressure.

Scenario 4 – RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE wait is increasing

Problem statement:

Application team reports that queries are taking longer than normal. Several sessions show RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE.

Possible reasons:

Queries requesting large memory grants.

Excessive concurrent queries.

Incorrect row estimates.

Memory grant overestimation.

Workspace memory pressure.

DBA action:

Execute:

SELECT
    session_id,
    requested_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS RequestedMemory_MB,
    granted_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS GrantedMemory_MB,
    used_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS UsedMemory_MB,
    wait_time_ms,
    wait_order
FROM sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants
ORDER BY requested_memory_kb DESC;

Resolution:

Find the queries with the largest grants.

Check actual versus estimated row counts.

Review hash and sort operators.

Investigate query memory grant feedback.

Optimize workload concurrency.

Scenario 5 – SQL Server memory consumption suddenly increases after deployment

Problem statement:

After a new application release, SQL Server memory utilization increases significantly.

Possible reasons:

New queries requesting larger memory grants.

New reporting operations.

Excessive ad hoc plan generation.

New data-processing workload.

Increased application concurrency.

A change in query execution plans.

Troubleshooting steps:

Compare memory usage before and after deployment.

Review Query Store.

Check memory grants.

Check memory clerks.

Review plan cache.

Identify newly introduced queries.

Compare execution plans.

Resolution:

Work with the application team to optimize the new workload and roll back or correct problematic changes when necessary.

Scenario 6 – SQL Server memory usage is low, but application is slow

Problem statement:

SQL Server memory utilization is low, but the application team reports slow performance.

Possible reasons:

Disk I/O bottleneck.

CPU bottleneck.

Blocking.

Network latency.

Query execution plan regression.

Application-side delays.

DBA action:

Check active requests:

SELECT
    session_id,
    status,
    wait_type,
    wait_time,
    blocking_session_id,
    cpu_time,
    total_elapsed_time,
    granted_query_memory
FROM sys.dm_exec_requests
WHERE session_id <> @@SPID
ORDER BY total_elapsed_time DESC;

Resolution: Identify the actual bottleneck. Do not increase RAM simply because an application is slow.

