Error 701 – Insufficient system memory

Error description:

SQL Server cannot allocate the memory required for an operation.

Possible reasons:

SQL Server memory pressure.

Excessive memory allocations.

Insufficient available memory.

Memory pool limitations.

External memory consumption.

DBA action:

Check SQL Server Error Log.

Check sys.dm_os_memory_clerks.

Check Windows available memory.

Check SQL Server process memory.

Review MAX Server Memory.

Investigate the component requesting memory.

Error 802 – Insufficient Buffer Pool Memory

Error description:

SQL Server cannot obtain sufficient memory from the Buffer Pool to complete an operation.

Possible reasons:

Buffer Pool memory pressure.

Insufficient available physical RAM.

Memory configuration issues.

Excessive memory requirements.

Resolution:

Check Buffer Pool consumption.

Review OS memory availability.

Identify memory-intensive queries.

Review memory configuration.

Consider increasing RAM if workload requirements justify it.

Error 8645 – Memory Grant Timeout

Important production error

Error description:

A query times out while waiting for memory resources required for execution.

Possible reasons:

Multiple concurrent memory-intensive queries.

Excessive memory grants.

Insufficient workspace memory.

Poor query execution plans.

DBA action:

Check the memory grant queue:

SELECT
    session_id,
    requested_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS RequestedMemory_MB,
    granted_memory_kb / 1024.0
        AS GrantedMemory_MB,
    wait_time_ms,
    wait_order
FROM sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants
ORDER BY wait_time_ms DESC;

Then review execution plans and query concurrency.

Error 17890 – SQL Server Process Memory Paged Out

Error description:

A significant part of SQL Server process memory has been paged out, potentially causing performance degradation.

Possible reasons:

Windows operating system memory pressure.

Incorrect memory allocation.

External applications competing for RAM.

SQL Server working-set trimming.

DBA action:

Check Windows available RAM.

Check SQL Server process memory.

Review MAX Server Memory.

Check SQL Server service account permissions.

Evaluate Lock Pages in Memory (LPIM).

Review operating system memory pressure.

Important: LPIM should be considered when there is evidence of SQL Server memory paging. It should not be enabled blindly. Ensure MAX Server Memory leaves sufficient memory for Windows and other allocations.

